      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE OF REVIEW",
          "content": "The goal of this review is to update the contributions of subclinical atherosclerosis imaging of coronary artery calcified plaque (CAC) to the primary prevention of coronary artery disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RECENT FINDINGS",
          "content": "Recent articles have increased support for the following: superiority of CAC determined risk to conventional risk factor-based paradigms, reclassification of risk by CAC, serial CAC scanning to assess the efficacy of therapy, CAC evaluation of high-risk groups (diabetes and other disease states characterized by inflammation), and redefinition of normal and abnormal lipids, ideal treatment goals and residual risk, as well as statin potency."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "SUMMARY",
          "content": "The paradigm shifts implicit in the supremacy of CAC herald a transformation in primary prevention from conventional risk factor paradigms to the evaluation of the disease itself by subclinical atherosclerosis imaging."
        }
      ]},
